WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.164
A decision is only as strong as the options under consideration.

2
00:00:04.164 --> 00:00:16.346
Many poor decisions start with too few options, often presented as a false binary: do this or do nothing, buy the tool or keep suffering, centralize everything or decentralize everything.

3
00:00:16.346 --> 00:00:18.351
Real choices are often richer.

4
00:00:18.351 --> 00:00:28.605
There may be combinations, staged approaches, experiments, temporary controls, or partial commitments that fit the situation better than either extreme.

5
00:00:28.605 --> 00:00:33.077
Generating realistic options requires creativity and discipline.

6
00:00:33.077 --> 00:00:37.549
Creativity helps the team avoid accepting the first visible answer.

7
00:00:37.549 --> 00:00:42.407
Discipline keeps the team from inventing options that cannot be executed.

8
00:00:42.407 --> 00:00:46.185
A useful question is, what are at least three viable paths?

9
00:00:46.185 --> 00:00:50.734
Another is, what would we do if our preferred option were unavailable?

10
00:00:50.734 --> 00:00:56.208
These prompts force the group to widen the option set without drifting into fantasy.

11
00:00:56.208 --> 00:01:00.064
Tradeoff analysis asks what each option gains and gives up.

12
00:01:00.064 --> 00:01:03.379
Opportunity cost is the value of what is not chosen.

13
00:01:03.379 --> 00:01:11.321
If a team spends two weeks hardening one system, those same people cannot spend those two weeks improving another control.

14
00:01:11.321 --> 00:01:15.870
If a leader chooses speed, they may give up some quality or resilience.

15
00:01:15.870 --> 00:01:20.650
If they choose a very robust process, they may give up flexibility or time.

16
00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:25.893
Decision makers also need to distinguish reversible and irreversible choices.

17
00:01:25.893 --> 00:01:30.905
A reversible choice can be tested, rolled back, or adjusted with modest cost.

18
00:01:30.905 --> 00:01:37.999
An irreversible or hard-to-reverse choice deserves more care because the downside of being wrong is larger.

19
00:01:37.999 --> 00:01:47.559
This distinction prevents teams from overanalyzing small experiments and underanalyzing commitments that lock in architecture, cost, or risk.

20
00:01:47.559 --> 00:01:50.875
Sometimes the best decision is not a single big bet.

21
00:01:50.875 --> 00:01:59.742
It may be a portfolio of options, a sequence of steps, a pilot, a staged rollout, or an experiment designed to produce better information.

22
00:01:59.742 --> 00:02:10.228
Satisficing, or choosing an option that is good enough under the constraints, may be wiser than endlessly optimizing when time is scarce and uncertainty is high.
